    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "In short: **no, you should not make something of the fact that the panel was made up entirely of women, nor is it on its own a reliable sign of bias or irregularity.** \n\nHere is an objective breakdown of why, what the decision likely reflects, and how you might handle the situation:\n\n---\n\n### 1. What the Panel Composition Means\n* **Commonality in Leadership / Demographics:** Depending on the firm’s executive structure and HR leadership, having an all-female panel may simply reflect who currently holds the relevant senior positions (e.g., HR Director, Operations Lead, Practice Director, Peer Group Head).\n* **Bias Implications:** Because both you and the successful candidate are women, gender discrimination is not a factor here. If your concern is potential age discrimination (e.g., 15 years of experience vs. 8 years), an all-female panel does not make age bias any more or less likely. Legally and practically, pointing to the gender makeup of the panel would not support a claim and would likely be viewed as an unfounded distraction.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Why the Other Candidate May Have Been Selected\nFor a **Head of Group** role at an engineering consultancy, the hiring criteria shift significantly away from pure technical delivery and toward commercial leadership:\n\n* **Technical Sufficiency vs. Commercial Impact:** At the shortlist stage, technical competence is typically taken as a given (both you and the candidate are Chartered). Once that baseline is established, decisions often hinge on:\n  * **Business Development & Revenue:** The announcement explicitly highlighted *\"excellent client relationships.\"* In consultancies, a Group Head is often expected to win work, retain key accounts, and drive profitability.\n  * **Leadership & Management Style:** How the candidate aligns with the firm’s current culture, growth strategy, or internal team dynamics.\n* **Experience Years as a Metric:** While 15 years of experience and leading three major projects is an impressive track record, consultancies often view 8+ years as sufficient for management if the candidate demonstrates exceptional commercial acumen, client pull, or strategic vision.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Two-Week Silence on Written Feedback\n* **HR Caution:** Asking for a formal *written* explanation often triggers caution in HR departments. They may be reviewing the wording with legal or senior leadership to ensure it does not expose the firm to disputes.\n* **Standard Delays:** Two weeks is a common timeframe for post-recruitment wrap-ups, contract negotiations, and internal onboarding, during which candidate feedback is frequently delayed.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Shift the Request from Formal Written Feedback to a Conversational Debrief:**\n   * Written feedback requests often put employers on the defensive. \n   * Consider sending a polite follow-up offering a brief 10–15 minute phone call:\n     > *\"I wanted to follow up on my request for feedback regarding the Head of Structural Engineering role. I really enjoyed meeting the team and remain very interested in future opportunities with [Company]. If easier than a written note, I would be grateful for a brief 10-minute call to hear any advice on areas I can strengthen for similar senior leadership roles in the future.\"*\n2. **Focus Questions on Commercial and Strategic Alignment:**\n   * If you secure a call, ask questions such as:\n     * *\"What specific strategic or commercial priorities tipped the balance in this selection?\"*\n     * *\"Were there particular client development or business growth areas where the panel felt other candidates stood out?\"*\n3. **Protect Your Professional Standing:**\n   * You reached the final shortlist of three for a department head position, which confirms your market value and leadership credentials. Keeping the relationship constructive and professional ensures you remain a top-of-mind candidate should another senior role arise or should their leadership needs evolve.\n",
